# Star 7 hp two-seater, 1902-1905.
#
# Star of Wolverhampton was one of the largest British car makers of the early
# 1900s, and the 7 hp "Little Star" was its volume model: a small single or twin
# cylinder two-seater, cheap enough for the professional buyer, and among the
# British light cars listed by The Autocar in 1904.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Motor_Company
#
# No works dimensions have been found. The model was built to these ESTIMATES:
# about 2.500 m long, 1.300 m wide, 1.700 m high, 1.700 m wheelbase. Correct
# them if a source is found.
#
# Colours: Dark Green, Blue, Maroon and Black match the surviving cars; the
# references show one in dark blue and one in green.
#
# Weight: 6 in total (green 2, blue 2, maroon 1, black 1). The 7 hp was a light
# car of the era, and the register treats early light cars as representative.
#
# Speed 40 km/h; comfort 25/215 (line). Class 2.
# Dates: 1902-1905 with a road tail to about 1925.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/star-7hp.blend. References:
# citycars/references/star-7hp/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia Commons.

obj=citycar
name=Star7hp(DarkGreen)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=2
intro_year=1902
intro_month=5
retire_year=1925
retire_month=6
speed=40
class=2
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=215

